9: The openmrsFetch function is a wrapper around the
10: [browser's built-in fetch function](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch),
11: with extra handling for OpenMRS-specific API behaviors, such as
12: request headers, authentication, authorization, and the API urls.
13: 
14: ## Type Parameters
15: 
16: ### T
17: 
18: `T` = `any`
19: 
20: ## Parameters
21: 
22: ### path
23: 
24: `string`
25: 
26: A string url to make the request to. Note that the
27:   openmrs base url (by default `/openmrs`) will be automatically
28:   prepended to the URL, so there is no need to include it.
29: 
30: ### fetchInit
31: 
32: [`FetchConfig`](../interfaces/FetchConfig.md) = `{}`
33: 
34: A [fetch init object](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/fetch#Syntax).
35:   Note that the `body` property does not need to be `JSON.stringify()`ed
36:   because openmrsFetch will do that for you.
37: 
38: ## Returns
39: 
40: `Promise`\<[`FetchResponse`](../interfaces/FetchResponse.md)\<`T`\>\>
41: 
42: A [Promise](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise)
43:   that resolves with a [Response object](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response).
44:   Note that the openmrs version of the Response object has already
45:   downloaded the HTTP response body as json, and has an additional
46:   `data` property with the HTTP response json as a javascript object.
47: 
48: ## Example
49: 
50: ```js
51: import { openmrsFetch } from '@openmrs/esm-api'
52: const abortController = new AbortController();
53: openmrsFetch(`${restBaseUrl}/session', {signal: abortController.signal})
54:   .then(response => {
55:     console.log(response.data.authenticated)
56:   })
57:   .catch(err => {
58:     console.error(err.status);
59:   })
60: abortController.abort();
61: openmrsFetch(`${restBaseUrl}/session', {
62:   method: 'POST',
63:   body: {
64:     username: 'hi',
65:     password: 'there',
66:   }
67: })
68: ```
